{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019f71CuVwoc7qzLQ1YBmWsH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd022-nl\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/nl: Number Lines of Files\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  macOS BSD nl differs from GNU nl in section delimiter handling and default formatting.\n6\t  Scripts that use nl for line numbering in structured documents rely on GNU nl's section\n7\t  delimiters and numbering style flags. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n8\t  reference binary (gnl, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations,\n9\t  verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n10\t\n11\tgoals:\n12\t  - G1: Number lines of each file with configurable style, format, and numbering range, matching GNU nl behavior.\n13\t  - G2: Support per-section numbering styles (body, header, footer) via -b, -h, and -f flags.\n14\t  - G3: Support numbering format options (-n ln/rn/rz), field width (-w), separator (-s), start value (-v), and increment (-i).\n15\t  - G4: \"Support logical page sections delimited by special lines (\\\\:\\\\:\\\\: header, \\\\:\\\\: body, \\\\: footer) and page delimiter (-d).\"\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Default line numbering\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: By default, must number non-empty lines in the body section only, using right-justified numbers in a field of width 6, with a tab character separating the number from the line content.\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: Empty lines (containing only a newline) in the body section must not be numbered by default. They must be passed through with no number and no separator.\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given. Must read each named file in argument order.\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: Line numbering must be continuous across files when multiple files are given (the counter does not reset between files unless a page delimiter is encountered).\n29\t\n30\t  R2:\n31\t    title: Numbering style flags (-b, -h, -f)\n32\t    items:\n33\t      - R2.1:\n34\t          text: \"-b STYLE must set the body section numbering style. Styles: a (number all lines), t (number non-empty lines, default), n (number no lines), p RE (number lines matching regular expression RE).\"\n35\t      - R2.2:\n36\t          text: \"-h STYLE must set the header section numbering style (default n). Same style values as -b.\"\n37\t      - R2.3:\n38\t          text: \"-f STYLE must set the footer section numbering style (default n). Same style values as -b.\"\n39\t      - R2.4:\n40\t          text: When the style is n for a section, lines in that section must pass through with no number and no separator character.\n41\t\n42\t  R3:\n43\t    title: Format and numbering options (-n, -w, -s, -v, -i, -l)\n44\t    items:\n45\t      - R3.1:\n46\t          text: \"-n FORMAT must set the line number format. Formats: ln (left-justified, no leading zeros), rn (right-justified, no leading zeros, default), rz (right-justified, leading zeros).\"\n47\t      - R3.2:\n48\t          text: -w N must set the line number field width to N characters (default 6).\n49\t      - R3.3:\n50\t          text: -s SEP must use SEP as the separator between the line number and the line content (default tab). SEP may be any string.\n51\t      - R3.4:\n52\t          text: -v N must set the initial line number to N (default 1). -i N must set the line number increment to N (default 1).\n53\t\n54\t  R4:\n55\t    title: Section delimiters and page reset (-d, -p, -l)\n56\t    items:\n57\t      - R4.1:\n58\t          text: \"A line containing only the two-character sequence \\\\:\\\\:\\\\: (the section delimiter repeated three times) marks the start of a header section. \\\\:\\\\: marks the start of a body section. \\\\: marks the start of a footer section. These delimiter lines are not written to output.\"\n59\t      - R4.2:\n60\t          text: Each new logical page (triggered by a header delimiter) resets the line counter to the value of -v unless -p is given.\n61\t      - R4.3:\n62\t          text: -p must suppress line counter reset at the start of each logical page; numbering continues from the previous page's last line number.\n63\t      - R4.4:\n64\t          text: -l N must treat N consecutive empty lines as a single empty line for the purpose of numbering with -b t. The default is 1 (each empty line counted separately). With -l 2, two or fewer consecutive empty lines are treated as one unnumbered block.\n65\t\n66\tnon_goals:\n67\t  - cmd/nl does not implement regex-based separator matching beyond the -p RE style argument.\n68\t  - cmd/nl does not implement locale-aware collation for -b p regex matching; Go regexp is used directly.\n69\t\n70\tacceptance_criteria:\n71\t  - id: AC1\n72\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | nl produces '     1\\\\ta\\\\n\\\\n     2\\\\tb\\\\n', matching gnl byte-for-byte.\"\n73\t    traces:\n74\t      - R1.1\n75\t      - R1.2\n76\t      - R1.3\n77\t      - R1.4\n78\t  - id: AC2\n79\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\n' | nl -b a -n ln -w 3 -s ': ' produces '1  : a\\\\n2  : b\\\\n', matching gnl.\"\n80\t    traces:\n81\t      - R2.1\n82\t      - R2.2\n83\t      - R2.3\n84\t      - R3.1\n85\t      - R3.2\n86\t      - R3.3\n87\t      - R4.4\n88\t  - id: AC3\n89\t    criterion: \"printf '\\\\\\\\:\\\\\\\\:\\\\\\\\:\\\\nheader\\\\n\\\\\\\\:\\\\\\\\:\\\\nbody line\\\\n\\\\\\\\:\\\\nbottom\\\\n' | nl -h a produces correct section-aware numbering, matching gnl.\"\n90\t    traces:\n91\t      - R2.2\n92\t  - id: AC4\n93\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\n' | nl -v 10 -i 5 produces '    10\\\\ta\\\\n    15\\\\tb\\\\n', matching gnl.\"\n94\t    traces:\n95\t      - R3.4\n96\t      - R4.2\n97\t  - id: AC5\n98\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gnl passes for all required test cases.\"\n99\t    traces:\n100\t      - R1.1\n101\t      - R1.2\n102\t      - R1.3\n103\t      - R1.4\n104\t  - id: AC6\n105\t    criterion: \"cmd/nl compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n106\t    traces:\n107\t      - R1.1\n108\t      - R1.2\n109\t      - R1.3\n110\t      - R1.4\n111\t      - R2.1\n112\t      - R2.2\n113\t      - R2.3\n114\t      - R2.4\n115\t      - R3.1\n116\t      - R3.2\n117\t      - R3.3\n118\t      - R3.4\n119\t      - R4.1\n120\t      - R4.2\n121\t      - R4.3\n122\t      - R4.4\n123\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51b64851-dd0b-4ec3-8464-a34c30fd28fa","uuid":"c26f0cf3-abd2-4c91-b74d-1152d346b54c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:23:34.308Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5255/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd022-nl.yaml","content":"id: srd022-nl\ntitle: \"cmd/nl: Number Lines of Files\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD nl differs from GNU nl in section delimiter handling and default formatting.\n  Scripts that use nl for line numbering in structured documents rely on GNU nl's section\n  delimiters and numbering style flags. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gnl, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations,\n  verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Number lines of each file with configurable style, format, and numbering range, matching GNU nl behavior.\n  - G2: Support per-section numbering styles (body, header, footer) via -b, -h, and -f flags.\n  - G3: Support numbering format options (-n ln/rn/rz), field width (-w), separator (-s), start value (-v), and increment (-i).\n  - G4: \"Support logical page sections delimited by special lines (\\\\:\\\\:\\\\: header, \\\\:\\\\: body, \\\\: footer) and page delimiter (-d).\"\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default line numbering\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: By default, must number non-empty lines in the body section only, using right-justified numbers in a field of width 6, with a tab character separating the number from the line content.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: Empty lines (containing only a newline) in the body section must not be numbered by default. They must be passed through with no number and no separator.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given. Must read each named file in argument order.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Line numbering must be continuous across files when multiple files are given (the counter does not reset between files unless a page delimiter is encountered).\n\n  R2:\n    title: Numbering style flags (-b, -h, -f)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: \"-b STYLE must set the body section numbering style. Styles: a (number all lines), t (number non-empty lines, default), n (number no lines), p RE (number lines matching regular expression RE).\"\n      - R2.2:\n          text: \"-h STYLE must set the header section numbering style (default n). Same style values as -b.\"\n      - R2.3:\n          text: \"-f STYLE must set the footer section numbering style (default n). Same style values as -b.\"\n      - R2.4:\n          text: When the style is n for a section, lines in that section must pass through with no number and no separator character.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Format and numbering options (-n, -w, -s, -v, -i, -l)\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: \"-n FORMAT must set the line number format. Formats: ln (left-justified, no leading zeros), rn (right-justified, no leading zeros, default), rz (right-justified, leading zeros).\"\n      - R3.2:\n          text: -w N must set the line number field width to N characters (default 6).\n      - R3.3:\n          text: -s SEP must use SEP as the separator between the line number and the line content (default tab). SEP may be any string.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: -v N must set the initial line number to N (default 1). -i N must set the line number increment to N (default 1).\n\n  R4:\n    title: Section delimiters and page reset (-d, -p, -l)\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: \"A line containing only the two-character sequence \\\\:\\\\:\\\\: (the section delimiter repeated three times) marks the start of a header section. \\\\:\\\\: marks the start of a body section. \\\\: marks the start of a footer section. These delimiter lines are not written to output.\"\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Each new logical page (triggered by a header delimiter) resets the line counter to the value of -v unless -p is given.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: -p must suppress line counter reset at the start of each logical page; numbering continues from the previous page's last line number.\n      - R4.4:\n          text: -l N must treat N consecutive empty lines as a single empty line for the purpose of numbering with -b t. The default is 1 (each empty line counted separately). With -l 2, two or fewer consecutive empty lines are treated as one unnumbered block.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/nl does not implement regex-based separator matching beyond the -p RE style argument.\n  - cmd/nl does not implement locale-aware collation for -b p regex matching; Go regexp is used directly.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | nl produces '     1\\\\ta\\\\n\\\\n     2\\\\tb\\\\n', matching gnl byte-for-byte.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\n' | nl -b a -n ln -w 3 -s ': ' produces '1  : a\\\\n2  : b\\\\n', matching gnl.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R4.4\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"printf '\\\\\\\\:\\\\\\\\:\\\\\\\\:\\\\nheader\\\\n\\\\\\\\:\\\\\\\\:\\\\nbody line\\\\n\\\\\\\\:\\\\nbottom\\\\n' | nl -h a produces correct section-aware numbering, matching gnl.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.2\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\n' | nl -v 10 -i 5 produces '    10\\\\ta\\\\n    15\\\\tb\\\\n', matching gnl.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.4\n      - R4.2\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gnl passes for all required test cases.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"cmd/nl compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R4.4\n","numLines":123,"startLine":1,"totalLines":123}}}
